  var chapters = [
    { title: "First Steps", chapter: "Start here · programs, print and running Python", line: 'print("Hello, world!")',
      content: {
        story: ["Python is a programming language — a way to write instructions a computer can follow. It was designed to read almost like English, which is why so many people start with it. Scientists, game makers, website builders and artists all use Python, from tiny scripts that tidy up a folder to the programs that train artificial intelligence.",
          "A Python program is a plain text file, usually ending in .py. The computer reads it from the top line to the bottom, one instruction at a time. The very first instruction most people learn is print, which shows text on the screen: print(\"Hello, world!\").",
          "To run a program you need the Python interpreter — the program that reads your code and carries it out. On most computers you type python3 main.py in a terminal. Websites like this one, and editors like IDLE, Thonny and VS Code, can run it for you with one button."],
        big: "A program is a list of instructions read top to bottom, and print is how a Python program talks to you.",
        caption: "The guide reads the line on the card and the screen says hello back. Tap Play to watch each step.",
        demo: ["Python opens main.py…", "Line 1: print(\"Hello, world!\")", "The screen shows: Hello, world!", "No more lines — the program ends."]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Python counts on tidiness. Each instruction sits on its own line, and lines that belong together are indented by the same amount. Other languages use curly braces or semicolons for this; Python uses the shape of the text itself, which is why Python code tends to look neat.",
          "Comments start with #. Python skips everything from # to the end of the line, so comments are notes for people. Good comments explain WHY the code does something — the code itself already shows what it does."],
        ex: { code: "# My first program\nprint(\"Hello, world!\")\nprint(\"I am learning Python.\")\nprint(2 + 3)",
          walk: ["Line 1: a comment — Python ignores it; it is a note for you.", "Line 2: print shows the text between the quotes.", "Line 3: a second print shows a second line, in order.", "Line 4: no quotes this time, so Python does the math first and prints 5."] },
        mistake: "Forgetting the quotes around text. print(Hello) makes Python look for a variable called Hello, and it stops with NameError: name 'Hello' is not defined. Text always needs quotes: print(\"Hello\")."
      } },
    { title: "Variables and Types", chapter: "Boxes with names · int, float, str, bool", line: "score = 0",
      content: {
        story: ["A variable is a name that points at a value, so you can use the value later. score = 0 creates the name score and points it at the number 0. Change it with another =, and the name points somewhere new.",
          "Every value has a type. Whole numbers are int, numbers with a decimal point are float, text is str (short for string), and True or False is bool. Python works the type out on its own — you never write it in front — and type(score) will tell you what it is.",
          "Names can use letters, digits and underscores, but cannot start with a digit, and capitals matter: Score and score are different names. Python programmers write several words joined by underscores, like high_score, a style called snake_case."],
        big: "A variable is a name for a value, and every value has a type: int, float, str or bool.",
        caption: "Four boxes, four types: a whole number, a decimal, some text and a yes-or-no. Tap Play to fill them.",
        demo: ["score = 0  → an int box", "price = 2.5  → a float box", "hero = \"Ana\"  → a str box", "alive = True  → a bool box"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Python's types matter when you mix them. 2 + 3 is 5, but \"2\" + \"3\" is \"23\" — adding two strings glues them together. Adding a string to a number, like \"Age: \" + 12, stops with a TypeError; turn the number into text first with str(12), or use an f-string.",
          "A name can be pointed at a value of a different type later (score = \"lots\"), and Python allows it. Just because you can doesn't mean you should: a name that keeps one kind of value is much easier to follow."],
        ex: { code: "score = 0\nprice = 2.5\nhero = \"Ana\"\nalive = True\nscore = score + 10\nprint(hero, score, price, alive)\nprint(type(price))",
          walk: ["Lines 1–4: four variables, each holding a different type of value.", "Line 5: read the right side first — 0 + 10 — then point score at the answer, 10.", "Line 6: print can take several values separated by commas; it puts a space between them: Ana 10 2.5 True.", "Line 7: type() reports the type: <class 'float'>."] },
        mistake: "Using a variable before giving it a value. print(total) on a line before total = 0 stops with NameError. Python reads from the top, so the box has to exist before you look inside it."
      } },
    { title: "Numbers and Math", chapter: "Calculating · + - * / // % ** and round", line: "total += price",
      content: {
        story: ["Python is a superb calculator. It uses + and - for adding and subtracting, * for multiplying and / for dividing. ** means “to the power of”: 2 ** 3 is 8. Brackets work just like in school math and are worked out first.",
          "Division has two flavours. / always gives a float: 7 / 2 is 3.5. // gives the whole-number part only (floor division): 7 // 2 is 3. And % gives the remainder: 7 % 2 is 1, which is perfect for asking whether a number is even.",
          "The shortcuts +=, -=, *= and /= change a variable using its own value: total += price adds price onto total. round(3.14159, 2) rounds to two decimal places, and abs(-5) gives 5."],
        big: "/ divides exactly, // keeps the whole part, % keeps the remainder, and ** raises to a power.",
        caption: "Seven cookies shared between two friends: each gets 3 (that's //), and 1 is left over (that's %).",
        demo: ["7 / 2   → 3.5", "7 // 2  → 3 each", "7 % 2   → 1 left over", "2 ** 3  → 8"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Floats are stored in binary, so some decimals can't be stored exactly: 0.1 + 0.2 shows 0.30000000000000004. That is normal in every language, not a Python bug. For money, count whole cents as ints, or use the decimal module.",
          "Python ints have no size limit — 2 ** 100 works perfectly and prints all 31 digits. The math module adds more tools: math.sqrt, math.pi, math.floor and math.ceil."],
        ex: { code: "cookies = 17\nfriends = 5\neach = cookies // friends\nleft = cookies % friends\nprint(f\"Each friend gets {each}, and {left} are left over.\")\nprint(2 ** 10)\nprint(round(7 / 3, 2))",
          walk: ["Lines 1–2: 17 cookies, 5 friends.", "Line 3: floor division — 17 // 5 is 3 whole cookies each.", "Line 4: the remainder — 17 % 5 is 2 left over.", "Line 5: an f-string puts both answers into one sentence.", "Line 6: 2 to the power of 10 is 1024.", "Line 7: 7 / 3 is 2.333…, rounded to two places: 2.33."] },
        mistake: "Expecting / to give a whole number. 10 / 2 is 5.0, a float, not 5. If you need an int for counting or for range(), use // or wrap the answer in int()."
      } },
    { title: "Strings", chapter: "Working with text · quotes, f-strings, methods, slices", line: 'print(f"Hi {name}!")',
      content: {
        story: ["A string is text: letters, digits, spaces and symbols in a row, wrapped in quotes. Single quotes and double quotes both work, as long as they match. Triple quotes (\"\"\" … \"\"\") let a string run over several lines.",
          "Strings come with built-in tools called methods, used with a dot: name.upper() shouts, name.lower() whispers, text.strip() trims spaces off the ends, and text.replace(\"cat\", \"dog\") swaps words. len(text) counts the characters.",
          "Every character has a position number, starting at 0. word[0] is the first letter and word[-1] the last. A slice like word[1:4] cuts out positions 1, 2 and 3. Strings can't be changed in place — methods always hand back a new string."],
        big: "Strings are text in quotes; f-strings fill in values, methods transform text, and slices cut pieces out.",
        caption: "The word is laid out on numbered tiles from 0. Tap Play to see a slice lift tiles 0 to 2.",
        demo: ["word = \"Python\"", "word[0] → \"P\"", "word[0:3] → \"Pyt\"", "word.upper() → \"PYTHON\"", "len(word) → 6"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["f-strings can do more than drop values in. {price:.2f} shows a float with exactly two decimals, {n:>5} pads a number to five characters wide, and {name!r} shows the value with its quotes — handy when debugging.",
          "Because strings never change in place, name.upper() on its own line does nothing you can see; you must keep the result: name = name.upper(). This is one of the most common surprises for new Python programmers."],
        ex: { code: "name = \"  ada lovelace  \"\nclean = name.strip().title()\nprint(clean)\nprint(len(clean))\nprint(clean[0:3])\nprint(f\"{clean} wrote the first program.\")\nprice = 4.5\nprint(f\"Price: ${price:.2f}\")",
          walk: ["Line 1: a messy name with spaces at both ends.", "Line 2: strip() trims the spaces, then title() capitalizes each word — methods can be chained.", "Line 3: prints Ada Lovelace.", "Line 4: 12 characters, including the space in the middle.", "Line 5: a slice of positions 0 to 2: Ada.", "Line 6: an f-string sentence with the name inside.", "Lines 7–8: :.2f formats 4.5 as 4.50."] },
        mistake: "Mixing quote types: \"It's fine\" works, but 'It's fine' ends the string at the apostrophe and Python reports a SyntaxError. Use the other kind of quote on the outside, or escape the inner one: 'It\\'s fine'."
      } },
    { title: "Decisions", chapter: "Choosing · if, elif, else and comparisons", line: "if hp <= 0:",
      content: {
        story: ["Programs make choices with if. After if comes a question that is either True or False, then a colon. The lines indented below run only when the answer is True. Unindent, and you are back in the main program.",
          "Questions are built from comparisons: == equal, != not equal, < less, > greater, <= less or equal, >= greater or equal. Combine them with and, or and not. Python even lets you chain them like math: 0 <= age < 18.",
          "elif (else if) asks another question when the earlier ones said no, and else catches everything left over. Python checks from the top and runs only the FIRST branch whose question is True — then skips the rest."],
        big: "if, elif and else pick ONE path: the first one whose question is True, or else as the fallback.",
        caption: "A fork in the road: the program looks at hp and takes exactly one path. Tap Play to see it choose.",
        demo: ["hp = 0", "if hp <= 0: → True!", "print(\"Game over\") runs", "the elif and else are skipped"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Some values count as False on their own: 0, an empty string \"\", an empty list [], and None. Everything else counts as True. So if bag: means “if the bag has anything in it” — short and very Pythonic.",
          "Since Python 3.10 there is also match, which compares one value against several patterns, a bit like switch in other languages. For simple choices, if and elif are still the everyday tool."],
        ex: { code: "temperature = 23\nif temperature >= 30:\n    print(\"Hot! Drink water.\")\nelif temperature >= 15:\n    print(\"Nice weather.\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Grab a jacket.\")\nprint(\"Have a good day!\")",
          walk: ["Line 1: today is 23 degrees.", "Line 2: is it 30 or more? No — skip its block.", "Line 4: is it 15 or more? Yes — run its block.", "Line 5: prints Nice weather.", "Line 6: else is skipped, because a branch already ran.", "Line 8: not indented, so it always runs: Have a good day!"] },
        mistake: "Using = instead of ==. if score = 10: is a SyntaxError — one = stores a value, two == ask a question. Python stops you before the program even starts, which is a gift: read the message, then add the second =."
      } },
    { title: "Loops", chapter: "Repeating · for, range, while, break and continue", line: "for i in range(5):",
      content: {
        story: ["Loops repeat work so you don't have to copy lines. A for loop walks through a collection one item at a time: for name in names: runs its indented block once for every name, with name holding the current one.",
          "range makes number sequences for counting loops. range(5) gives 0 to 4, range(1, 6) gives 1 to 5, and range(10, 0, -1) counts down from 10 to 1. The stop number is never included — that surprises everyone once.",
          "A while loop keeps going as long as its question is True, which suits “repeat until” jobs where you don't know the count. break leaves a loop at once, and continue skips straight to the next round."],
        big: "for repeats once per item in a collection; while repeats as long as a question stays True.",
        caption: "The counter walks along five stepping stones, 0 to 4, doing the same job on each. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["for i in range(5):", "i = 0 → job done", "i = 1 → job done", "… i = 4 → job done", "range is used up — loop ends"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["enumerate gives you the position and the item together: for i, name in enumerate(names): — no need to count by hand. zip walks two lists side by side: for name, score in zip(names, scores):.",
          "A while loop needs something inside it that eventually makes the question False. If nothing changes, the loop never ends; in a terminal, Ctrl+C stops a runaway program."],
        ex: { code: "for n in range(1, 6):\n    print(n, \"squared is\", n * n)\n\nlives = 3\nwhile lives > 0:\n    print(\"Lives left:\", lives)\n    lives -= 1\nprint(\"Game over\")",
          walk: ["Line 1: n takes the values 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.", "Line 2: prints each number and its square.", "Line 4: we start with 3 lives.", "Line 5: keep going while lives is above 0.", "Lines 6–7: print, then lose a life — this change is what lets the loop finish.", "Line 8: runs once the loop is over."] },
        mistake: "Off-by-one with range: range(1, 10) stops at 9, not 10. If you want 1 to 10 including 10, write range(1, 11). Think of the stop number as “up to, but not including.”"
      } },
    { title: "Lists and Tuples", chapter: "Collections in order · lists, indexes, methods, tuples", line: "scores = [10, 20, 30]",
      content: {
        story: ["A list holds many values in order: scores = [10, 20, 30]. Reach an item by position with square brackets, starting at 0: scores[0] is 10, and scores[-1] is the last item. len(scores) tells you how many there are.",
          "Lists change. append adds to the end, insert adds at a position, remove deletes a value, pop takes the last item out and hands it to you, and sort puts everything in order. scores[1] = 25 replaces an item.",
          "A tuple is like a list that can't change, written with round brackets: point = (3, 4). Use tuples for small fixed groups — a coordinate, a date, an RGB color — where changing one part by accident would be a bug."],
        big: "Lists are ordered and changeable; tuples are ordered and fixed. Both count positions from 0.",
        caption: "A train of numbered carriages: append hooks a new one on the end. Tap Play to watch it grow.",
        demo: ["scores = [10, 20, 30]", "scores.append(40)", "scores → [10, 20, 30, 40]", "scores[0] → 10, scores[-1] → 40"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Assigning a list to another name does NOT copy it: b = a gives the same list two names, so changing b changes a. To get a separate copy, use b = a.copy() or b = list(a).",
          "sorted(scores) hands back a new sorted list and leaves the original alone, while scores.sort() sorts the list itself and returns None. Both take reverse=True to sort from biggest to smallest."],
        ex: { code: "scores = [40, 10, 30]\nscores.append(20)\nscores.sort()\nprint(scores)\nprint(\"Best:\", scores[-1])\nprint(\"How many:\", len(scores))\npoint = (3, 4)\nx, y = point\nprint(x + y)",
          walk: ["Line 1: a list of three scores.", "Line 2: append adds 20 to the end.", "Line 3: sort arranges the list itself in order.", "Line 4: [10, 20, 30, 40].", "Line 5: the last item is the best: 40.", "Line 6: four scores.", "Lines 7–9: a tuple unpacked into x and y; 3 + 4 prints 7."] },
        mistake: "Reaching past the end. A list of 4 items has positions 0 to 3, so scores[4] stops with IndexError: list index out of range. Use scores[-1] for the last item, or check len(scores) first."
      } },
    { title: "Dictionaries and Sets", chapter: "Looking things up · keys, values, items, sets", line: 'ages = {"Ana": 12, "Leo": 14}',
      content: {
        story: ["A dictionary (dict) stores labeled values: each key points at a value. ages = {\"Ana\": 12, \"Leo\": 14}. You look up by key, not by position: ages[\"Leo\"] is 14. Adding is just as easy: ages[\"Mia\"] = 11.",
          "Dictionaries are the Swiss Army knife of Python: a phone book, a game's inventory, a word counter, the settings of an app. Loop through them with for name, age in ages.items():, or check a key with if \"Ana\" in ages:.",
          "A set is a bag of unique values with no order: {\"red\", \"blue\"}. Adding something already inside does nothing. Sets are perfect for removing duplicates and for fast “have I seen this?” questions."],
        big: "A dictionary finds values by key; a set keeps each value once. Both answer “is this in here?” very fast.",
        caption: "A wall of labeled drawers: open the one marked Leo and find 14 inside. Tap Play to look things up.",
        demo: ["ages = {\"Ana\": 12, \"Leo\": 14}", "ages[\"Leo\"] → 14", "ages[\"Mia\"] = 11 adds a drawer", "\"Ana\" in ages → True"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Looking up a key that isn't there stops with KeyError. ages.get(\"Zed\") returns None instead, and ages.get(\"Zed\", 0) returns 0 — a safe default. Counting things is a classic use: counts[word] = counts.get(word, 0) + 1.",
          "Since Python 3.7, dictionaries remember the order keys were added in, so looping over one gives keys in insertion order. Sets still have no order you can rely on."],
        ex: { code: "inventory = {\"apples\": 3, \"pears\": 0}\ninventory[\"plums\"] = 5\ninventory[\"apples\"] += 2\nfor fruit, count in inventory.items():\n    print(f\"{fruit}: {count}\")\ncolors = {\"red\", \"blue\", \"red\"}\nprint(len(colors))",
          walk: ["Line 1: a dictionary of fruit counts.", "Line 2: a new key, plums, with 5.", "Line 3: the apples count goes from 3 to 5.", "Lines 4–5: items() hands back each key and value together; three lines print.", "Line 6: the duplicate red is dropped.", "Line 7: so the set holds 2 values."] },
        mistake: "Using a list where a dictionary belongs: searching a list of [name, score] pairs by hand. If you keep asking “what is the X for this Y?”, Y should be a dictionary key."
      } },
    { title: "Functions", chapter: "Building machines · def, parameters, return, scope", line: "def damage(atk, guard):",
      content: {
        story: ["A function is a named, reusable machine. You build it once with def, then call it whenever you need it by writing its name with brackets. Functions keep programs short and organized: each one does one job, with a clear name.",
          "Values go in through parameters — the names in the brackets. When you call damage(10, 3), atk becomes 10 and guard becomes 3 inside the function. return sends the answer back out, and the call is replaced by that answer.",
          "Variables made inside a function live only inside it; this is called scope. Two functions can each have a variable called total and never collide. To share results, return them rather than reaching outside."],
        big: "def builds a reusable machine: parameters carry values in, return carries the answer out.",
        caption: "Numbers drop into the machine's funnel, gears turn, and the answer slides out of return. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["def damage(atk, guard):", "    return atk - guard", "damage(10, 3) is called", "atk = 10, guard = 3", "the call becomes 7"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Arguments can be passed by name: damage(guard=3, atk=10) does the same as damage(10, 3), and names make long calls easier to read. Defaults (def greet(name=\"friend\")) let callers skip arguments they don't care about.",
          "A docstring — a string on the first line of a function — explains what it does, and help(function) shows it. Type hints like def damage(atk: int, guard: int) -> int: document the expected types without changing how the code runs."],
        ex: { code: "def damage(atk, guard):\n    \"\"\"Hit points lost after the guard blocks some.\"\"\"\n    hit = atk - guard\n    return max(hit, 0)\n\nprint(damage(10, 3))\nprint(damage(2, 5))\nprint(damage(guard=1, atk=4))",
          walk: ["Line 1: def names the function and its two parameters.", "Line 2: a docstring explaining the machine.", "Line 3: work out the raw hit.", "Line 4: max keeps the answer from going below 0.", "Line 6: 10 - 3 → prints 7.", "Line 7: 2 - 5 is -3, but max makes it 0.", "Line 8: arguments by name — prints 3."] },
        mistake: "Printing instead of returning. A function that prints its answer can't hand it to the rest of the program: x = add(2, 3) gets None. Return the value, and let the caller decide whether to print it."
      } },
    { title: "Errors and Exceptions", chapter: "When things go wrong · tracebacks, try, except, raise", line: "except ValueError as err:",
      content: {
        story: ["Errors are normal, and Python explains them well. When something goes wrong, it prints a traceback: the lines it was running, and at the bottom the error type and message, like ValueError: invalid literal for int(). Read from the bottom up.",
          "Some errors can be expected — a user types \"ten\" instead of 10, or a file is missing. Put the risky line under try:, and the rescue plan under except SomeError:. If the error happens, Python jumps to except instead of crashing.",
          "else: runs when no error happened, and finally: runs no matter what — perfect for tidying up. And you can raise errors yourself to stop bad data early: raise ValueError(\"age can't be negative\")."],
        big: "A traceback tells you what broke and where; try and except let a program recover from errors it expects.",
        caption: "A tightrope walker over a safety net: if int() slips, except catches the fall. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["try: age = int(\"ten\")", "ValueError is raised!", "except ValueError: catches it", "print(\"Please type digits\")", "the program keeps running"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Catch the narrowest error you expect. A bare except: catches everything — including typos in your own code and the Ctrl+C that tries to stop the program — and hides real bugs. except ValueError: says exactly what you're ready for.",
          "Common built-in errors: NameError (unknown name), TypeError (wrong type, like \"a\" + 1), ValueError (right type, bad value), IndexError and KeyError (missing position or key), ZeroDivisionError and FileNotFoundError."],
        ex: { code: "answers = [\"12\", \"ten\", \"7\"]\nfor text in answers:\n    try:\n        number = int(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        print(f\"'{text}' is not a whole number\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"Got {number}\")\n    finally:\n        print(\"checked\", text)",
          walk: ["Line 1: three answers, one of them in words.", "Line 2: check each answer in turn.", "Lines 3–4: the risky line — int() may raise ValueError.", "Lines 5–6: for \"ten\", Python jumps here and explains.", "Lines 7–8: else runs only when int() worked.", "Lines 9–10: finally runs every time, error or not."] },
        mistake: "Reading the traceback from the top. The top shows where the program started; the cause is at the BOTTOM — the last line names the error, and the line just above shows the code that raised it."
      } },
    { title: "Files", chapter: "Saving and loading · open, with, read, write", line: 'with open("save.txt", "w") as f:',
      content: {
        story: ["Variables vanish when a program ends; files remember. open(\"save.txt\", \"w\") opens a file for writing (creating it if needed), \"r\" opens it for reading, and \"a\" for adding to the end without erasing what's there.",
          "Always open files with with: with open(\"save.txt\", \"w\") as f:. Everything indented underneath can use f, and the moment the block ends Python closes the file properly — even if an error happens halfway.",
          "f.write(text) writes text (add \"\\n\" for new lines), f.read() reads the whole file as one string, and for line in f: reads it line by line — the best way for big files."],
        big: "with open(...) as f: opens a file safely; \"w\" writes, \"a\" adds, \"r\" reads, and the file closes by itself.",
        caption: "A notebook is opened, a line is written, and the cover closes by itself at the end. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["with open(\"save.txt\", \"w\") as f:", "    f.write(\"level 3\\n\")", "the block ends → file closed", "with open(\"save.txt\") as f:", "    f.read() → \"level 3\\n\""]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Paths can be tricky across Windows, macOS and Linux. The pathlib module makes them easy: Path(\"saves\") / \"game.txt\" builds the right path everywhere, and Path(\"game.txt\").exists() checks before you read.",
          "For structured data, don't invent your own format. The json module saves dictionaries and lists as text — json.dump(data, f) — and loads them back with json.load(f). The csv module reads and writes spreadsheets."],
        ex: { code: "with open(\"save.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"level 3\\n\")\n    f.write(\"coins 120\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"save.txt\") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        print(line.strip())",
          walk: ["Line 1: open save.txt for writing; the file is created.", "Lines 2–3: two lines of text, each ending in \\n.", "Line 5: open it again — \"r\" (read) is the default.", "Line 6: walk through the file one line at a time.", "Line 7: strip() removes the line ending before printing."] },
        mistake: "Opening with \"w\" when you meant \"a\". Write mode empties the file the moment it opens, so a save file opened with \"w\" just to add one score loses everything before it. Use \"a\" to append."
      } },
    { title: "Classes and Objects", chapter: "Your own types · class, __init__, self, methods", line: "class Player:",
      content: {
        story: ["A class is a blueprint, and objects are the things built from it. class Player: describes what every player has (a name, health) and can do (take damage, heal). hero = Player(\"Ana\") builds one actual player from the blueprint.",
          "__init__ is the set-up function that runs whenever a new object is built. Its first parameter, self, is the brand-new object, and lines like self.hp = 100 give it its own data. Data stored on self is called an attribute.",
          "Functions inside a class are called methods, and they also take self first, so they can reach the object's own data. hero.heal(10) runs heal with self set to hero — Python fills self in for you."],
        big: "A class is a blueprint; each object built from it has its own data (attributes) and actions (methods).",
        caption: "One blueprint, three robots stamped out — each with its own name and battery. Tap Play to build them.",
        demo: ["class Player: (the blueprint)", "hero = Player(\"Ana\")", "__init__ sets hero.hp = 100", "hero.heal(10) → hp 110"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Every object is separate. Two players built from the same class have their own hp, so hurting one never hurts the other. That independence is the whole point of classes.",
          "Python's dataclasses module can write __init__ for you: put @dataclass above a class and list its fields with types. It's the quickest way to make classes that mostly hold data."],
        ex: { code: "class Player:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.hp = 100\n\n    def hurt(self, amount):\n        self.hp -= amount\n        print(f\"{self.name} has {self.hp} hp\")\n\nhero = Player(\"Ana\")\nsidekick = Player(\"Leo\")\nhero.hurt(30)\nsidekick.hurt(5)",
          walk: ["Line 1: the blueprint is named Player.", "Lines 2–4: set-up — every new player gets a name and 100 hp.", "Lines 6–8: a method that lowers this player's hp and reports it.", "Lines 10–11: two separate players are built.", "Line 12: Ana has 70 hp.", "Line 13: Leo has 95 hp — Ana's hit never touched him."] },
        mistake: "Forgetting self. Writing hp = 100 instead of self.hp = 100 inside __init__ makes a temporary variable that disappears when the function ends, and later hero.hp stops with AttributeError."
      } },
    { title: "Inheritance and Special Methods", chapter: "Family trees · child classes, super(), __str__", line: "class Wizard(Player):",
      content: {
        story: ["Inheritance builds a new class on top of an old one. class Wizard(Player): makes Wizard a child of Player: it gets every attribute and method Player has, for free, and can add its own — like mana and cast_spell.",
          "A child can also replace a parent's method by defining one with the same name; this is called overriding. Inside it, super() reaches the parent's version, so super().__init__(name) runs Player's set-up before the wizard adds its own.",
          "Methods with double underscores, like __init__, are special methods Python calls for you. __str__ decides what print(obj) shows, __len__ answers len(obj), and __eq__ decides what == means for your objects."],
        big: "A child class inherits everything from its parent, can add more, and can override; super() calls the parent's version.",
        caption: "A family tree: Wizard and Knight both grow from Player, each adding a branch of its own. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["class Wizard(Player):", "inherits name, hp, hurt()", "adds self.mana = 50", "print(wizard) uses __str__"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Use inheritance for “is a” relationships: a Wizard IS a Player. For “has a” relationships — a Player HAS a backpack — store one object inside another instead. This second idea is called composition, and it's often the simpler choice.",
          "isinstance(obj, Player) checks whether an object was built from Player or any of its children; it's the polite way to ask an object what it is."],
        ex: { code: "class Player:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.hp = 100\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"{self.name} ({self.hp} hp)\"\n\nclass Wizard(Player):\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        super().__init__(name)\n        self.mana = 50\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return super().__str__() + f\" with {self.mana} mana\"\n\nmerlin = Wizard(\"Merlin\")\nprint(merlin)\nprint(isinstance(merlin, Player))",
          walk: ["Lines 1–4: the parent class sets a name and hp.", "Lines 6–7: __str__ decides how a Player prints.", "Line 9: Wizard is a child of Player.", "Lines 10–12: super().__init__ runs the parent set-up, then mana is added.", "Lines 14–15: the child's __str__ builds on the parent's.", "Line 18: prints Merlin (100 hp) with 50 mana.", "Line 19: True — a Wizard is a Player."] },
        mistake: "Forgetting super().__init__(...) in a child's __init__. The parent's set-up never runs, so self.name and self.hp are never created, and the first method that uses them stops with AttributeError."
      } },
    { title: "Modules and Packages", chapter: "Borrowing tools · import, the standard library, pip", line: "import random",
      content: {
        story: ["A module is a Python file full of ready-made tools. import random brings in the random module, and random.randint(1, 6) uses one of its tools. Python comes with a huge standard library: math, random, datetime, json, csv, pathlib and hundreds more.",
          "You can import just one tool with from math import sqrt, then call sqrt(16) directly. Your own .py files are modules too: put helper functions in helpers.py, then import helpers in main.py.",
          "Beyond the standard library, the Python Package Index holds hundreds of thousands of free packages, installed with pip install name. Use a virtual environment (python3 -m venv .venv) so each project keeps its own set."],
        big: "import borrows tools from modules — Python's own standard library, your files, or packages installed with pip.",
        caption: "A toolbox shelf: import pulls down the random box, and randint rolls a die. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["import random", "random.randint(1, 6) → 4", "from math import sqrt", "sqrt(16) → 4.0"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Every module has a __name__. When a file is run directly, its __name__ is \"__main__\"; when it's imported, it's the module's name. That's why programs end with if __name__ == \"__main__\": main() — the main code runs when you run the file, but not when another file imports it.",
          "Never name your own file after a module you use. A file called random.py in your folder hides the real random module, and import random suddenly finds the wrong one."],
        ex: { code: "import math\nfrom datetime import date\n\ndef main():\n    print(math.sqrt(81))\n    print(math.pi > 3)\n    print(date(2000, 1, 1).year)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()",
          walk: ["Line 1: the whole math module.", "Line 2: just one tool, date, from datetime.", "Lines 4–7: the program's main work lives in a function.", "Line 5: the square root of 81 is 9.0.", "Line 6: pi is about 3.14, so True.", "Line 7: prints 2000.", "Lines 9–10: run main only when this file is run directly."] },
        mistake: "Calling a module's tool without the module name. After import random, randint(1, 6) on its own stops with NameError; write random.randint(1, 6), or import the tool itself with from random import randint."
      } },
    { title: "Comprehensions and Generators", chapter: "Pythonic power · comprehensions, iterators, yield", line: "squares = [n * n for n in range(5)]",
      content: {
        story: ["A comprehension builds a collection in one readable line. [n * n for n in range(5)] means “n times n, for every n in range(5)” and gives [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]. Add a filter at the end: [n for n in nums if n > 0] keeps only the positives.",
          "Comprehensions exist for dictionaries and sets too: {name: len(name) for name in names} pairs each name with its length. They replace three or four lines of loop-and-append with one clear line — but if a comprehension gets hard to read, a normal loop is better.",
          "A generator produces values one at a time instead of building a whole list. Write it like a function, but use yield instead of return. Generators can be infinite, and they use almost no memory, because each value is made only when asked for."],
        big: "Comprehensions build lists, dicts and sets in one line; generators yield values one at a time, on demand.",
        caption: "A conveyor belt: numbers ride in, get squared at the machine, and drop into a new list. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["[n * n for n in range(5)]", "0 → 0, 1 → 1, 2 → 4", "3 → 9, 4 → 16", "→ [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Round brackets instead of square ones make a generator expression: sum(n * n for n in range(1000000)) adds a million squares without ever storing a million-item list.",
          "Anything you can loop over is an iterable — lists, strings, dicts, files, ranges and generators. That shared idea is why the same for loop works on all of them."],
        ex: { code: "nums = [4, -2, 7, 0, -5]\npositives = [n for n in nums if n > 0]\nprint(positives)\nlengths = {word: len(word) for word in [\"hi\", \"hello\"]}\nprint(lengths)\n\ndef countdown(start):\n    while start > 0:\n        yield start\n        start -= 1\n\nprint(list(countdown(3)))",
          walk: ["Line 1: a list with positives, negatives and zero.", "Line 2: keep n only if it is above 0.", "Line 3: [4, 7].", "Line 4: a dict comprehension: each word with its length.", "Line 5: {'hi': 2, 'hello': 5}.", "Lines 7–10: a generator yields 3, then 2, then 1.", "Line 12: list() collects them: [3, 2, 1]."] },
        mistake: "Cramming too much into one comprehension. Two loops and two conditions on one line may work, but nobody — including future you — can read it. When it stops being obvious, write the loop out."
      } },
    { title: "Working with Data", chapter: "Real-world data · json, csv, sorting, counting", line: "data = json.loads(text)",
      content: {
        story: ["Most real programs work with data: scores, prices, survey answers, weather readings. Python's standard library already handles the common formats. JSON is how websites send data, and json.loads turns JSON text into dictionaries and lists.",
          "Spreadsheets travel as CSV files — values separated by commas. csv.DictReader reads each row as a dictionary keyed by the header row, so row[\"price\"] reads like plain English.",
          "Once data is in lists and dicts, Python's built-ins do the heavy lifting: sum, min, max, len, sorted with key=, and collections.Counter for counting. Larger projects reach for packages like pandas, but the basics take you surprisingly far."],
        big: "json and csv turn text into lists and dicts, and sum, max, sorted and Counter answer questions about them.",
        caption: "Raw text flows in, becomes a neat table, and a chart pops up from the answers. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["text = '{\"Ana\": 12, \"Leo\": 15}'", "data = json.loads(text)", "max(data, key=data.get) → \"Leo\"", "sum(data.values()) → 27"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["sorted(items, key=...) sorts by anything: key=len sorts words by length, and key=lambda row: row[\"price\"] sorts rows by price. Add reverse=True for biggest first.",
          "json.dumps(data, indent=2) turns data back into neatly indented JSON text — the same format web APIs use, which makes saving and sharing data easy."],
        ex: { code: "import json\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ntext = '{\"Ana\": 12, \"Leo\": 15, \"Mia\": 9}'\nscores = json.loads(text)\nbest = max(scores, key=scores.get)\nprint(best, scores[best])\nprint(sorted(scores.values()))\nvotes = Counter([\"red\", \"blue\", \"red\"])\nprint(votes.most_common(1))",
          walk: ["Lines 1–2: two standard-library tools.", "Line 4: JSON text, as a website might send it.", "Line 5: json.loads turns it into a dictionary.", "Line 6: the key with the biggest value.", "Line 7: prints Leo 15.", "Line 8: the scores in order: [9, 12, 15].", "Lines 9–10: Counter tallies votes: [('red', 2)]."] },
        mistake: "Treating numbers from a CSV as numbers. Everything csv reads is text, so \"9\" > \"12\" is True (text compares letter by letter). Convert with int() or float() before doing math or sorting."
      } }
  ];

  var shelves = [
    { title: "Standard Library Field Guide", chapter: "Beyond the basics · Python shelf", line: ["import math, random, datetime", "from pathlib import Path"],
      content: {
        story: ["Python's motto is “batteries included”: it ships with a standard library of tools for almost every everyday job. Before writing something yourself — or installing a package — check whether the standard library already has it.",
          "math does math beyond + and *, random picks and shuffles, datetime handles dates and times, pathlib works with files and folders, json and csv read and write data, and collections adds smarter containers like Counter and defaultdict.",
          "The official documentation at docs.python.org lists every module with examples. Learning a handful of them well is worth more than knowing hundreds of names."],
        big: "Check the standard library first: math, random, datetime, pathlib, json, csv and collections cover most daily jobs.",
        caption: "A workshop wall of labeled toolboxes, each one ready to borrow with import. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["math.sqrt(49) → 7.0", "random.choice([\"a\", \"b\"])", "date.today()", "Path(\"notes.txt\").exists()"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["collections.defaultdict(list) creates missing keys automatically with an empty list, so grouping things needs no if-checks: groups[key].append(item) just works.",
          "itertools holds tools for clever looping — combinations, permutations, chaining lists together — and statistics computes mean, median and mode in one call."],
        ex: { code: "from collections import defaultdict\nimport statistics\n\nwords = [\"apple\", \"avocado\", \"banana\", \"blueberry\", \"cherry\"]\nby_letter = defaultdict(list)\nfor w in words:\n    by_letter[w[0]].append(w)\nprint(dict(by_letter))\nprint(statistics.mean([3, 5, 10]))",
          walk: ["Lines 1–2: two standard-library modules.", "Line 4: five fruit words.", "Line 5: a dictionary that makes an empty list for any new key.", "Lines 6–7: group each word under its first letter.", "Line 8: {'a': [...], 'b': [...], 'c': [...]}.", "Line 9: the mean of 3, 5 and 10 is 6."] },
        mistake: "Installing a package for something built in. Before pip install, search the standard library — dates, JSON, CSV, random numbers and paths are all already there, tested and documented."
      } },
    { title: "The Bug Hunter's Handbook", chapter: "Beyond the basics · Python shelf", line: ["print(f\"{x=}\")", "breakpoint()"],
      content: {
        story: ["Every programmer writes bugs; good programmers find them calmly. Step one is always to read the error message from the bottom: it names the error type and the exact line. Step two: make the problem happen again on purpose, with the smallest input you can.",
          "The quickest tool is print. f\"{x=}\" prints both the name and the value, like x=5, so you can watch values change as the program runs. When print isn't enough, breakpoint() pauses the program and opens the debugger, where you can inspect every variable.",
          "Most bugs come from a short list: a typo in a name, the wrong indentation, an off-by-one in range, = where == was meant, text where a number was expected, or a function that prints instead of returning."],
        big: "Read the error from the bottom, reproduce it small, then use print(f\"{x=}\") or breakpoint() to watch the values.",
        caption: "A detective follows footprints backwards from the crash to the line that caused it. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["Traceback (most recent call last):", "  File \"main.py\", line 4", "TypeError: can only concatenate str", "→ line 4 adds text to a number"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Rubber-duck debugging is a real technique: explain your code, line by line, out loud to someone — or to a rubber duck. Saying what each line SHOULD do usually reveals the one that doesn't.",
          "Write tiny tests for things you've fixed. A test is just code that checks an answer: assert add(2, 2) == 4. If a fix ever breaks again, the test will tell you immediately."],
        ex: { code: "def average(nums):\n    return sum(nums) / len(nums)\n\nmarks = [8, 9, 10]\nresult = average(marks)\nprint(f\"{result=}\")\nassert result == 9\nprint(\"all checks passed\")",
          walk: ["Lines 1–2: a small function to test.", "Line 4: some sample data.", "Line 5: call the function.", "Line 6: {result=} prints result=9.0 — name and value together.", "Line 7: assert stops the program loudly if the answer is ever wrong.", "Line 8: reached only when the check passes."] },
        mistake: "Changing many things at once. Change one thing, run, look. If you change five things and the bug disappears, you won't know which change fixed it — or which one created the next bug."
      } },
    { title: "Pythonic Patterns", chapter: "Beyond the basics · Python shelf", line: ["for i, item in enumerate(items):", "a, b = b, a"],
      content: {
        story: ["“Pythonic” code uses the language the way it was designed to be used: clear, short and readable. Python even has a poem about it — type import this to read The Zen of Python. “Readability counts” is one of its lines.",
          "Some favorite patterns: swap two variables with a, b = b, a. Loop with enumerate instead of counting by hand. Use in to check membership. Use with for files. Use f-strings for text. Use comprehensions for simple transformations.",
          "Python style is written down in PEP 8: four spaces per indent, snake_case for names, CapWords for classes, spaces around operators, lines that aren't too long. Tools like black and ruff can tidy code automatically."],
        big: "Pythonic code is readable code: enumerate, unpacking, in, with, f-strings and comprehensions, written PEP 8 style.",
        caption: "Two versions of the same program side by side: the long way, and the Pythonic way. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["i = 0; while i < len(x): …  (long)", "for i, item in enumerate(x):  (Pythonic)", "temp = a; a = b; b = temp  (long)", "a, b = b, a  (Pythonic)"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["“It's easier to ask forgiveness than permission” is a Python habit: instead of checking every condition first, try the action and handle the exception if it fails. It often makes code shorter and avoids race conditions.",
          "any() and all() answer questions about whole collections: any(n < 0 for n in nums) is True if at least one number is negative; all(...) only if every item passes."],
        ex: { code: "names = [\"Ana\", \"Leo\", \"Mia\"]\nfor place, name in enumerate(names, start=1):\n    print(place, name)\na, b = 1, 2\na, b = b, a\nprint(a, b)\nprint(any(len(n) > 3 for n in names))\nprint(\", \".join(names))",
          walk: ["Line 1: a list of names.", "Lines 2–3: enumerate counts for us, starting at 1.", "Lines 4–5: swap without a temporary variable.", "Line 6: prints 2 1.", "Line 7: is any name longer than 3 letters? False.", "Line 8: join glues the names with commas: Ana, Leo, Mia."] },
        mistake: "Writing Java or C in Python: for i in range(len(names)): print(names[i]). It works, but for name in names: print(name) is shorter, clearer and harder to get wrong."
      } },
    { title: "Automate the Boring Stuff", chapter: "Beyond the basics · Python shelf", line: ["for path in Path(\".\").glob(\"*.txt\"):", "time.sleep(1)"],
      content: {
        story: ["Python shines at automation: letting the computer do repetitive jobs for you. Renaming 500 photos, tidying a downloads folder, adding up numbers from many files, or sending yourself a reminder — each is a few lines of Python.",
          "pathlib walks folders: Path(\".\").glob(\"*.txt\") finds every text file. shutil copies and moves files. datetime stamps things with dates, and time.sleep pauses between steps.",
          "Always test automation on a copy first, and print what the program WOULD do before letting it actually rename or delete anything. A loop that makes the same mistake 500 times is still a mistake."],
        big: "A few lines of Python can do hours of repetitive work — but test on copies and print before you change files.",
        caption: "A little robot sorts a messy pile of files into neat labeled folders. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["for path in Path(\".\").glob(\"*.txt\"):", "    print(\"would move\", path.name)", "…check the list first…", "then move them for real"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["Command-line arguments make scripts reusable: sys.argv holds the words typed after the script name, and the argparse module turns them into proper options with help text.",
          "Schedule scripts to run by themselves with your system's scheduler — cron on Linux and macOS, Task Scheduler on Windows — so the boring job happens every morning without you."],
        ex: { code: "from pathlib import Path\n\nfolder = Path(\"demo_notes\")\nfolder.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nfor n in range(3):\n    (folder / f\"note{n}.txt\").write_text(f\"note number {n}\")\nfor path in sorted(folder.glob(\"*.txt\")):\n    print(path.name, len(path.read_text()), \"characters\")",
          walk: ["Line 1: pathlib's Path type.", "Lines 3–4: a folder, created if it doesn't exist yet.", "Lines 5–6: write three small files; / joins folder and file name.", "Line 7: find every .txt file in the folder, in order.", "Line 8: print each file's name and length."] },
        mistake: "Running destructive automation on real files first. Delete and rename loops can't be undone; do a dry run that only prints, check it, then run it for real — ideally on a backup."
      } },
    { title: "Testing with pytest", chapter: "Beyond the basics · Python shelf", line: ["def test_add():", "    assert add(2, 2) == 4"],
      content: {
        story: ["A test is a small program that checks your program. You write the answer you expect, run the code, and compare. When every test passes, you can change code with confidence; when one fails, it points straight at what broke.",
          "pytest is the most popular Python testing tool (pip install pytest). Put tests in files named test_something.py, write functions whose names start with test_, and use plain assert to check answers. Run pytest and it finds and runs them all.",
          "Good tests are small, fast and specific: one function, one situation, one expected answer. Test the normal case, the edge cases (zero, empty, the biggest value) and the error cases."],
        big: "Tests are code that checks code: name functions test_…, assert the expected answer, and run pytest.",
        caption: "A checklist on a clipboard: each test gets a green tick as pytest runs it. Tap Play.",
        demo: ["$ pytest", "test_math.py ...", "3 passed in 0.01s", "✓ every check is green"]
      },
      deep: {
        more: ["pytest.raises checks that bad input raises the right error: with pytest.raises(ValueError): parse_age(\"-1\"). Testing the failures matters as much as testing the successes.",
          "Writing a test BEFORE fixing a bug proves the bug exists, and proves the fix works. That habit — reproduce, test, fix — keeps old bugs from ever coming back."],
        ex: { code: "def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\ndef test_add():\n    assert add(2, 2) == 4\n    assert add(-1, 1) == 0\n    assert add(\"a\", \"b\") == \"ab\"\n\ntest_add()\nprint(\"tests passed\")",
          walk: ["Lines 1–2: the code under test.", "Line 4: pytest finds functions whose names start with test_.", "Lines 5–7: three checks — normal, edge, and text.", "Line 9: here we call the test by hand so the example runs without pytest.", "Line 10: reached only if every assert passed."] },
        mistake: "Tests that test nothing. A test that never asserts — or asserts something always true — passes forever while bugs walk right past it. Every test needs at least one real check of a real answer."
      } }
  ];

